 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 30,
   "text": "TOWER BRIDGE. Not London Bridge! That one is plainer. This one has two towers and a road that lifts for tall ships. In 1952 a double-decker bus jumped the gap while it was opening. The bus was fine."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "THE THAMES. Britain's most famous river, with over 30 bridges in London alone. Once so smelly that Parliament hung soaked curtains at the windows, now clean enough for seals and the occasional confused porpoise."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "PALACE OF WESTMINSTER. Where the nation argues about the nation. The clock tower is the Elizabeth Tower; the bell inside is Big Ben, named after either Sir Benjamin Hall or a boxer called Ben Caunt."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "BIG BEN CLOCK. The clock keeps time by a pile of old pennies on the pendulum. To speed it up, add a penny. London has its priorities."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "THE UNDERGROUND. The oldest metro in the world, opened 1863. The map is a diagram, not a map, because Harry Beck knew nobody cares where anything really is. They care about where to change. The mice are unionised."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "THE VANE TOWER. Lord Vane's glass headquarters: 'a monument to a more orderly countryside'. The revolving door has never once let anyone in. It does, in fairness, rotate with great enthusiasm."
  },
  {
   "x": 70,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "THE TOWER OF LONDON. A palace, a fortress, a prison and a treasury, with the Crown Jewels and a lot of stairs. The Yeoman Warders, or Beefeaters, live inside, give excellent tours, and will tell you off for petting a raven."
  },
  {
   "x": 73,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "THE RAVENS. If the ravens leave the Tower, it is said, the kingdom will fall. So there are always at least six, their wings are gently trimmed, and they are very well fed. They are also rather smug about all of it."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 51,
   "text": "SHAKESPEARE'S GLOBE. A faithful rebuild of the 1599 theatre, with a thatched roof and a yard where the 'groundlings' stand. The original burned down in 1613, after a stage cannon went off a bit enthusiastically."
  },
  {
   "x": 48,
   "y": 51,
   "text": "THE SHARD. The tallest building in Western Europe when it opened in 2012: 310 metres of glass, shaped like a shard of glass, which is nicely honest. Please do not throw anything at it. You will be on camera."
  },
  {
   "x": 62,
   "y": 57,
   "text": "THE LONDON EYE. A big wheel by the Thames. A ride takes 30 minutes and rises 135 metres. You can see Windsor Castle on a clear day, and your own breath on a cold one."
  },
  {
   "x": 57,
   "y": 60,
   "text": "BOROUGH MARKET. A market has stood near here for a thousand years. You can buy cheese, bread, oysters, and a sausage roll bigger than your head. London food has come a very long way since the pie."
  }
 ],
